// int32 multiplication must trap even though the exact product fits in 64 bits.
//
// This is the case a fast path is most likely to get wrong: computing the product in a
// wider native type succeeds, so the trap can only come from checking the result against
// the *declared* width afterwards. Multiply is also the operation where the intermediate
// is furthest from the operands, so a check on the operands rather than the result would
// pass.

function main():void {
    print("before\n");
    int32 big = 65536;
    int32 product = big * big;
    print(product);
    eof: pass;
}
